Previously, `SYCL_KERNEL_ASSERT` invoked the compiler's built-in function `__assert_fail` in the Linux path, but the SYCL compiler does not guarantee support for this functionality. As a result, this "implement your own assertion" approach bypasses the `NDEBUG` constraint, causing assertions to remain enabled regardless. By switching to standard C++ assertions, we ensure that `SYCL_KERNEL_ASSERT` adheres to the `NDEBUG` directive.

A simple reproduce
```cpp
#include <cassert>
#include <sycl/sycl.hpp>

using namespace sycl;

extern "C" {
extern SYCL_EXTERNAL void __assert_fail(
    const char* expr,
    const char* file,
    unsigned int line,
    const char* func);
}

#define SYCL_KERNEL_ASSERT(cond) \
  if (!(cond)) __assert_fail(#cond, __FILE__, static_cast<unsigned int>(__LINE__), __func__);
// #define SYCL_KERNEL_ASSERT(cond) assert(cond)

void user_func(item<2> Item) { SYCL_KERNEL_ASSERT(!(Item[0] % 2) && "Nil"); }

int main() {
  queue Q;
  Q.submit([&](handler &CGH) {
    CGH.parallel_for<class TheKernel>(range<2>{2, 1},
                                      [=](item<2> It) { user_func(It); });
  });
  Q.wait();
  std::cout << "One shouldn`t see this message." << std::endl;
  return 0;
}
```
This demo is not constrained by `NDEBUG`; it will always abort when the assertion condition is met.

